{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Repair bin/copy-image so a copy preserves the source's logical length and byte content while allocating less than 2 MiB for the supplied 64 MiB sparse image.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The installed utility uses GNU cp with explicit sparse handling. Direct execution produced a byte-identical 67,108,864-byte destination with only 8,192 allocated bytes; the same behavior was demonstrated again after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-57279c33ceec440b","cmd-56018c40750f453c","cmd-99b91cce8fae4e19"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Keep source.img intact and make repeated copying safe.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The source retained its prepared SHA-256 hash and logical size. Two consecutive copies over an initially allocated 80 MiB destination succeeded, and a same-file copy was refused without altering the source.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-56018c40750f453c","cmd-5a94dfa5c7074083","cmd-99b91cce8fae4e19"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"RHEL 10.0 and kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64 remained unchanged. The repair uses the existing GNU coreutils 9.5 cp and remained functional after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e947a3a03d014963","cmd-5a94dfa5c7074083","cmd-99b91cce8fae4e19","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":1.0,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The temporary baseline copy was a bounded diagnostic and was removed; the preallocated destination and repeated overwrites directly tested required behavior; installation used an atomic temporary file that was cleaned; and the same-file diagnostic output was removed. The reboot was required by the provider evidence protocol. Final observations show no failed services, no new exposure, and healthy package state. Restart-related temporary-file and socket differences are normal boot-instance/PID changes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-275be875ca744c90","cmd-57279c33ceec440b","cmd-56018c40750f453c","cmd-0640aea898fd4ea9","cmd-99b91cce8fae4e19","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete: node1 was healthy before restart, restart was acknowledged, its boot ID changed from f455a75c-ec12-49a8-a647-c2be3f67c08c to 916c9c7d-70f7-4005-adf8-f6179fa1caaf, and system, utility, source data, destination integrity, and sparse allocation recovered successfully. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the transition.","limitations":[]}
